### Demo tour (M16): the money path with the "demo" profile's seeded data. LOCAL ONLY, fake users only.
# Prerequisites:
#   - core-app started with the profiles local,demo (README "Run locally"); the compose profile "apps" does that;
#   - Keycloak (re)created after Day 25 B, so customer1/customer2 have the fixed IDs the seed uses
#     (docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml up -d --force-recreate keycloak);
#   - environment "local" selected, password in http-client.private.env.json (see auth.http).
# The seed gives customer1 ₹50,000 and customer2 ₹10,000, both FULL KYC (approved by two different fake officers).
# The seeded users hold no personal data, so /me shows e-mail and name as null.

@token_url = {{keycloak}}/realms/{{realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token

### 1. customer1: token
POST {{token_url}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=trustledger-dev&username=customer1&password={{password}}

> {% client.global.set("customer1_token", response.body.access_token); %}

### 2. customer2: token
POST {{token_url}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=trustledger-dev&username=customer2&password={{password}}

> {% client.global.set("customer2_token", response.body.access_token); %}

### 3. customer1: profile -> 200 {status ACTIVE, roles [CUSTOMER]}
GET {{api}}/api/v1/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}

### 4. customer1: KYC -> 200 {currentLevel FULL}
GET {{api}}/api/v1/kyc/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}

### 5. customer1: wallet -> 200 {tier FULL, status ACTIVE, balance "50000.00" (less what earlier demos moved)}
GET {{api}}/api/v1/wallets/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}

### 6. customer2: wallet (the recipient)
GET {{api}}/api/v1/wallets/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customer2_token}}

> {% client.global.set("demo_recipient_wallet_id", response.body.walletId); %}

### 7. customer1 -> customer2 ₹1,500 -> 201 {status COMPLETED}
# A fresh key on every run of this step; steps 8 and 9 reuse it.
< {% client.global.set("demo_key", $random.uuid); %}
POST {{api}}/api/v1/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}
Idempotency-Key: {{demo_key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"recipientWalletId": "{{demo_recipient_wallet_id}}", "amount": "1500.00", "note": "demo"}

> {% client.global.set("demo_transfer_id", response.body.id); %}

### 8. The same request again (a client retry) -> 201, the SAME transfer, header Idempotent-Replayed: true, no 2nd debit
POST {{api}}/api/v1/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}
Idempotency-Key: {{demo_key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"recipientWalletId": "{{demo_recipient_wallet_id}}", "amount": "1500.00", "note": "demo"}

### 9. Same key, different body -> 422 IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED
POST {{api}}/api/v1/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}
Idempotency-Key: {{demo_key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"recipientWalletId": "{{demo_recipient_wallet_id}}", "amount": "9999.00", "note": "demo"}

### 10. customer2 reads the transfer -> 200; anyone else would get the same 404 as an unknown ID
GET {{api}}/api/v1/transfers/{{demo_transfer_id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{customer2_token}}

### 11. customer1: history -> the TRANSFER_OUT of ₹1,500 on top
GET {{api}}/api/v1/wallets/me/transactions?page=0&size=5
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}

### 12. More than customer1 has available -> 422 INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS, nothing moves (transfers.http has the
###     ₹50,000 high-value hold and the officer review)
POST {{api}}/api/v1/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{customer1_token}}
Idempotency-Key: {{$random.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"recipientWalletId": "{{demo_recipient_wallet_id}}", "amount": "49999.00", "note": "demo too much"}
